Mind if I walk with you?

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:24.960
Clearly, but this is where murder said he'd meet us.

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:36.700
You know, Freud said, the idea of dying during or just after the sex act has an ambivalent appeal.

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:40.760
You see, men, for the most part, have a desire for it, but on the other hand, they fear it.

00:24:41.210 --> 00:24:43.590
It's true, like, there is such a thing as psychosomatic death.

00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:49.740
Okay, let's say that the first two deaths really were genuine cardiac arrests, okay?

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:53.370
Now, who knows what dormant, ironic fantasies that would stir up?

00:24:54.110 --> 00:24:57.290
You know what we might have here? Mass hysteria.

00:24:58.170 --> 00:24:59.770
You don't really believe any of that, do you?

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:02.230
No.

00:25:30.250 --> 00:25:31.070
Just a cop before.

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:17.200
It's Dr. Merger.

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:18.780
We're killing him.

00:26:26.990 --> 00:26:27.210
Peters.

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:54.240
Yeah, I'm fine. Thanks, baby.

00:26:54.900 --> 00:26:59.970
Agar. Yeah, find a plumber. They're what's left of the...

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.660
The thing where the bridge is out on the old highway.

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:02.380
Empty.

00:27:02.790 --> 00:27:03.230
Empty?

00:27:04.210 --> 00:27:04.850
Makes nine.

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:07.060
Makes ten.

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:08.800
Ten.

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:13.390
Barney Braddock.

00:27:13.690 --> 00:27:17.500
We found him with his pants around his ankles, dead of a massive you-know-what.

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:24.020
All right, all right.

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:28.840
Natives are getting restless.

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:31.070
Yeah, I don't blame her.

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:35.310
Look, take some of your men and find out whose car that was, will you?

00:27:35.660 --> 00:27:36.800
Yeah, I already figured on doing that.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:41.420
I know you government heroes don't expect us local yokels to be able to do anything by ourselves,

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:42.500
but we get along all right.

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:49.160
Look, Peters, I know you got a job. I got a job, too.

00:27:49.500 --> 00:27:49.680
Right.

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:51.830
There are now three men dead at Brandt.

00:27:54.430 --> 00:27:59.610
Hey, Peters, Peters, I'm trying to say I'm sorry if I've been stepping on your toes.

00:28:00.970 --> 00:28:05.010
I'm sorry, too. We're uptight. You're uptight. I'm uptight. We're all uptight.

00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:06.520
Really?

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:10.490
They're droppin' like flies, they go. Droppin' like flies.

00:28:17.870 --> 00:28:19.410
Wiggles and wiggles and wiggles.

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:21.490
It's got a motor. It's got a motor.

00:28:34.090 --> 00:28:39.210
Hello, Operator. I want area code 202, State Department 415.

00:28:40.440 --> 00:28:43.360
Let's go, officers. Everybody. Oh, come on now. It's all off.

00:28:44.350 --> 00:28:57.570
Just call me back here. Area code 805-259-9035. That's right, 9035. Thanks.

00:29:02.620 --> 00:29:04.040
Hey, it looks like this is going to take a while.

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:05.500
Why don't you wait in the car?

00:29:06.270 --> 00:29:07.490
Okay. Where is it?

00:29:08.080 --> 00:29:08.920
The lot right behind.

00:29:09.740 --> 00:29:10.120
All right.

00:29:12.210 --> 00:29:12.970
Be with you in a minute.

00:29:19.480 --> 00:29:19.700
Yellow.

00:29:21.130 --> 00:29:22.790
Uh, yes, Neil Agar here.

00:29:24.260 --> 00:29:24.960
Mr. Secretary?

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:28.550
Yes, sir. I'm sorry to wake you up, sir.

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:43.340
If you take a thinker's dozen, that's what I do.

00:29:43.960 --> 00:29:47.690
Just want to play rough, huh? Is that it?

00:29:50.300 --> 00:29:51.500
Get her, get her, get her, get her.

00:29:52.060 --> 00:29:53.800
That's it. She's a beautiful one.

00:29:54.620 --> 00:29:56.700
Don't juggle, baby. Don't fight her.

00:29:57.770 --> 00:29:58.670
Yeah, come on, come on.

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:08.010
Yes, I know, Merger's death wasn't like the others, but that doesn't necessarily rule out a pattern.

00:30:10.870 --> 00:30:11.450
Yes, I know, sir.

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:16.980
I don't know why, but I'm sure it's connected with Brant.

00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:24.880
Get you some of that.

00:30:25.700 --> 00:30:26.860
Look at it, warm it up.

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:39.300
Yes, I'd recommend that we put up a military quarantine and cordon off this community.

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:47.340
Right, sir. Thank you, sir. I'll keep you appraised of any further developments.

00:30:49.350 --> 00:30:51.020
Right. Good night, sir.

00:31:01.660 --> 00:31:05.520
Or maybe they just might cut you open and examine you as a medical phenomenon.

00:31:06.780 --> 00:31:07.980
Now, what do you think of that?

00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:31.320
Thank you.

00:34:40.270 --> 00:34:40.490
I don't know.

00:35:00.510 --> 00:35:02.060
What the fuck?

00:35:32.970 --> 00:35:34.550
I was in town tonight when they found the body.

00:35:35.290 --> 00:35:38.820
But when I got out of the shock, all I could think of was all the letters he had for me.

00:35:40.580 --> 00:35:41.260
The rest is history.

00:35:42.930 --> 00:35:43.410
How'd you meet?

00:35:44.730 --> 00:35:46.010
I drive a truck at the cannery.

00:35:46.150 --> 00:35:47.170
We met at Herm's bar.

00:35:48.460 --> 00:35:51.280
He used to come in there a lot before we settled down.

00:35:54.110 --> 00:35:55.570
Where were you tonight when Merger was killed?

00:35:56.570 --> 00:35:57.410
At Herm's, honest.

00:35:57.590 --> 00:35:58.250
I've got witnesses.

00:36:00.120 --> 00:36:02.940
I was supposed to see Henry tonight, but he telephoned and called it off.

00:36:03.220 --> 00:36:05.670
I was angry. I was hurt.

00:36:07.260 --> 00:36:08.180
So you went over to her?

00:36:13.350 --> 00:36:16.070
You don't have any idea where Murder was tonight?

00:36:16.310 --> 00:36:16.850
None at all?

00:36:17.450 --> 00:36:19.790
He wasn't with a man. He was with a girl.

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:22.420
I saw them as they drove through town earlier this evening.

00:36:23.330 --> 00:36:24.090
What'd she look like?

00:36:26.830 --> 00:36:29.490
It was too dark. She was driving his car. That's all I could make out.

00:36:31.240 --> 00:36:31.960
You're sure, Joe?

00:36:33.900 --> 00:36:35.840
What the hell you think made me notice in the first place?

00:36:41.340 --> 00:36:42.340
Tell me something, will you?

00:36:44.720 --> 00:36:45.700
Could he make it with a woman?

00:37:00.640 --> 00:37:01.160
Thank you.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:38.120
How are you, Dr. Haas?

00:37:38.960 --> 00:37:39.840
Susan will do.

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:43.940
Where is everybody?

00:37:47.040 --> 00:37:48.700
Locked in their rooms, I shouldn't wonder.

00:37:49.360 --> 00:37:53.340
Being thrilled at the latest chapters of the Phantom of the Room Morgue.

00:37:53.720 --> 00:37:54.240
And you?

00:37:56.010 --> 00:37:59.970
I deal in principles of geometry, trigonometry, as in certain...

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:08.090
I'm afraid abstraction is more Dr. Williams' line, not mine.

00:38:10.530 --> 00:38:12.990
I seem to crave nothing but sweets of late.

00:38:17.200 --> 00:38:17.620
Too bad.

00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:21.980
What's too bad about it, Herbert?

00:38:23.600 --> 00:38:25.540
Well, you know, that's very bad for your appetite.

00:38:26.720 --> 00:38:29.980
You see, I was going to...

00:38:30.090 --> 00:38:37.570
ask you if you would have dinner with me tonight. I mean, that is if you're free. Oh, I'm quite free

00:38:37.570 --> 00:38:46.650
as a matter of fact. You mean you will? Well, I must admit that since the curfew, Peckham's been

00:38:46.650 --> 00:38:55.550
more boring than usual, but I don't see why that should apply to us. Do you? I mean, as long as

00:38:55.550 --> 00:38:56.210
we're discreet.

00:39:01.940 --> 00:39:06.840
Great. Then I'll cook you up something extra special.

00:39:13.030 --> 00:39:14.210
No scuttlebutt around here?

00:39:14.960 --> 00:39:18.780
Well, there was a rumor about Julie and him.

00:39:18.960 --> 00:39:21.480
It was just a rumor. Not that anyone took that seriously.

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:25.080
How well did you know murder?

00:39:25.990 --> 00:39:29.720
How well did anyone know murder? An introverted recluse.

00:39:30.030 --> 00:39:30.770
What can I tell you?

00:39:32.670 --> 00:39:35.340
You, uh, you going to Grabowski's funeral tomorrow?

00:39:35.580 --> 00:39:35.920
Yeah.

00:39:37.900 --> 00:39:39.380
Well, he was a fine scientist.

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:50.180
Yes, please.

00:40:00.250 --> 00:40:00.890
Hiya, darling.

00:40:04.990 --> 00:40:11.210
Now listen, I've got a summary of recovery operations tacked up on the board,

00:40:11.210 --> 00:40:17.890
so I'm afraid I'm going to be nosed down and ass up for another couple of hours, so curfew.

00:40:20.670 --> 00:40:23.450
For God's sakes, Nora, you're an eternal pessimist. Relax.

00:40:25.260 --> 00:40:26.260
Now what should happen?

00:40:28.070 --> 00:40:29.490
Listen, darling, I gotta run.

00:40:31.180 --> 00:40:33.400
No. No, don't wait up, okay?

00:40:35.990 --> 00:40:36.730
Yeah, tell me later.

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:41.170
Uh-huh. I love you, too.

00:40:46.260 --> 00:40:47.700
Domestic bliss, Mr. Agar.

00:40:47.780 --> 00:40:49.240
They gotta know where you are every second.

00:40:57.360 --> 00:40:58.920
I'm, uh, meeting Julie Zorn.

00:40:59.360 --> 00:40:59.660
Ooh.

00:41:00.250 --> 00:41:03.410
Cute girl. She'd only let her hair down. Much too serious.

00:41:03.790 --> 00:41:06.980
Wish me luck.

00:41:07.760 --> 00:41:08.080
Good luck.

00:41:08.470 --> 00:41:11.630
About to explore the Antarctic and see what's beneath all that ice.

00:41:12.580 --> 00:41:25.320
Seems to be working all right.

00:41:26.460 --> 00:41:28.360
Now, tell me again what it is you're looking for.

00:41:30.760 --> 00:41:38.830
It's just something Peter said. They're dropping like flies. It's just a long shot. Let's go.

00:41:41.100 --> 00:41:44.540
Insects outnumber in species all the rest of the animal kingdom.

00:41:45.740 --> 00:41:50.060
Insects represent the highest grade of evolution among invertebrate animals,

00:41:50.700 --> 00:41:55.350
not only in terms of their complexity of structure, but also in their psychic development.

00:42:17.400 --> 00:42:18.360
You mind if I smoke?

00:42:18.460 --> 00:42:20.120
No, I'm allergic to cigarette smoke.

00:42:21.520 --> 00:42:23.330
Oh, well, just a bad...

00:42:23.330 --> 00:42:24.950
Sure you wouldn't like some dessert?

00:42:27.160 --> 00:42:28.640
No, I don't think so.

00:42:28.740 --> 00:42:29.920
See, I've got to watch my...

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:31.020
Just a taste?

00:42:34.110 --> 00:42:38.240
Well, just a...

00:42:53.010 --> 00:42:53.670
Have a taste?

00:43:06.010 --> 00:43:14.830
The visual organs effects consist of compound eyes.

00:43:15.720 --> 00:43:23.500
The entire image an insect sees, consequently, is a mosaic made up of as many units as there are facets to the cornea.

00:43:24.600 --> 00:43:28.180
Multiple vision occurs throughout the entire insect world.

00:44:20.730 --> 00:44:29.740
metamorphosis of the insect to its adult state occurs within the pupa. Finally, the worker bees tear open the milky white pupa allowing

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:37.100
the queen to emerge. Here the queen has been marked with a red spot. The survival of a hive

00:44:37.100 --> 00:44:44.800
depends upon a fertile queen. Many queens are developed until one can be made fertile. During

00:44:44.800 --> 00:44:48.300
her lifetime, queen will lay up to 20 million eggs.

00:45:01.400 --> 00:45:01.800
Thank you.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:34.420
Examples of the female killing the male during mating are numerous in the insect world.

00:46:35.070 --> 00:46:40.280
Here, the black widow awaits the arrival of her mate, and if the male, upon sexual contact,

00:46:40.560 --> 00:46:43.120
is not extremely nimble, his fate is sealed.

00:46:43.970 --> 00:46:49.910
The female praying mantis not only kills the male upon mating, but even proceeds to devour him.

00:46:50.760 --> 00:46:53.100
Similarly, the queen bee mates in mid-flight.

00:47:36.540 --> 00:47:36.700
We'll be right back.

00:49:34.700 --> 00:49:35.000
Thank you.

00:50:44.910 --> 00:50:45.910
I have on hand.

00:50:52.500 --> 00:50:52.980
Yes.

00:50:55.580 --> 00:50:55.880
Yes.

00:51:00.410 --> 00:51:02.630
Who is this?

00:51:02.850 --> 00:51:06.090
Just do exactly as I say, or you won't ever see him alive again.

00:51:06.250 --> 00:51:06.750
Do you understand?

00:51:10.110 --> 00:51:10.430
Yes.

00:51:11.920 --> 00:51:15.400
There's some sort of social pattern here, but I just can't figure out what it is.

00:51:17.590 --> 00:51:20.610
Professor Nordstent's the head of the etymological department here, isn't he?

00:51:21.450 --> 00:51:24.880
Yeah, but he's away in Europe. Susan Harris is acting head while he's gone.

00:51:26.270 --> 00:51:29.150
I think I should have a talk with her. Thank you.

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:30.720
Why don't you go on home?

00:51:31.470 --> 00:51:31.810
But...

00:51:31.810 --> 00:51:32.490
No buts.

00:51:32.880 --> 00:51:33.840
You've done enough for one.

00:52:19.420 --> 00:52:21.620
This is Neil Agar, State Department Security.

00:52:22.716 --> 00:52:25.196
I'd like to speak to Dr. Harris, if she's there, please.

00:52:25.820 --> 00:52:28.440
This is Dr. Harris. I'm sorry, but I'm very busy right now.

00:52:30.284 --> 00:52:31.444
regular office hours.

00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:34.780
No, it's quite important that I see you now.

00:52:36.428 --> 00:52:36.988
All right.

00:52:37.548 --> 00:52:38.408
I'll be out in a moment.

00:53:07.676 --> 00:53:09.676
Hello, Dr. Harris.

00:53:10.556 --> 00:53:12.576
Look, I have a few questions I'd like to ask you.

00:53:12.596 --> 00:53:14.016
Well, I'm afraid that's impossible.

00:53:14.216 --> 00:53:16.636
We're in the middle of a very important experiment.

00:53:17.212 --> 00:53:18.252
Oui, Dr. Harris?

00:53:19.676 --> 00:53:20.916
Dr. Norstad and I.

00:53:21.724 --> 00:53:22.644
Well, then he's here.

00:53:23.292 --> 00:53:23.972
He's in London.

00:53:24.668 --> 00:53:26.788
at an entomological society convention.

00:53:27.356 --> 00:53:28.516
I'm working from his notes.

00:53:28.924 --> 00:53:29.404
Oh, I see.

00:53:32.972 --> 00:53:33.572
Mr. Edgar.

00:53:35.084 --> 00:53:37.744
I'll answer anything you like. Just please be as brief as possible.

00:53:37.744 --> 00:53:39.004
I'm in the middle of an experiment.

00:53:39.692 --> 00:53:40.592
Dr. Harris.

00:53:43.404 --> 00:53:45.264
How many times does a queen bee meet?

00:53:47.052 --> 00:53:48.052
Only once.

00:53:50.412 --> 00:53:50.832
Just once.

00:53:52.172 --> 00:53:53.372
and only to reproduce.

00:53:53.900 --> 00:53:55.680
Once accomplished, she never mates again.

00:54:00.156 --> 00:54:01.296
Yes, thank you, Doctor.

00:54:43.180 --> 00:54:43.520
All right.

01:04:00.220 --> 01:04:02.020
You still able to recognize him as Herb Klein?

01:04:03.410 --> 01:04:03.830
Positively.

01:04:05.140 --> 01:04:06.220
I'd better go tell his wife.

01:04:06.990 --> 01:04:08.170
You feel like coming along?

01:04:09.080 --> 01:04:10.880
Uh, no, you take care of it.

01:04:11.360 --> 01:04:12.100
I'll be your brand.

01:04:33.320 --> 01:04:42.620
Hey, what's the trouble?

01:04:42.860 --> 01:04:43.900
Military quarantine, sir.

01:04:44.640 --> 01:04:45.560
You mean I can't get through?

01:04:45.720 --> 01:04:47.580
Orders are to seal off the entire community, sir.

01:04:48.240 --> 01:04:51.540
But this is... Hey, listen, I've got the chair of seminar at UCLA this afternoon.

01:04:51.700 --> 01:04:54.140
Sorry, sir. Our orders are quite specific. No one goes out or in.

01:04:54.600 --> 01:04:56.880
Oh, this is ridiculous. Who ordered this cordon?

01:04:57.120 --> 01:04:58.120
Secretary of State, sir.

01:05:12.250 --> 01:05:12.790
Captain Peters.

01:05:13.230 --> 01:05:13.790
Mr. Klein?

01:05:14.200 --> 01:05:15.300
Yes, come in.

01:05:15.760 --> 01:05:16.100
Come in.

01:05:26.360 --> 01:05:27.220
May I get you something?

01:05:28.020 --> 01:05:29.000
Coffee or juice?

01:05:29.280 --> 01:05:29.980
Oh, no, no, no.

01:05:30.060 --> 01:05:33.680
Oh, thank you. Actually, I've got some bad news for you, Mrs. Klein.

01:05:34.460 --> 01:05:39.960
At this hour of the morning, that would be considered shameful.

01:05:42.550 --> 01:05:44.980
Sit down, at least. Please.

01:05:57.690 --> 01:05:59.050
Her husband is dead, Mrs. Klein.

01:06:01.210 --> 01:06:01.930
Herb dead?

01:06:02.420 --> 01:06:05.140
Yes, we found his body at the factory this morning.

01:06:05.880 --> 01:06:09.630
The coroner should be calling in about a half an hour to ask you to identify the body.

01:06:10.670 --> 01:06:16.100
I don't seem shocked, Captain Peters.

01:06:16.260 --> 01:06:20.940
It's only because his passing comes as no great surprise.

01:06:23.160 --> 01:06:28.040
Dear Herb never was what you would call a cautious man.

01:06:33.360 --> 01:06:37.550
I'm sorry to be the bearer of such bad news, Mrs. Klein.

01:06:42.680 --> 01:06:43.800
Nothing else for me to do here.

01:06:45.760 --> 01:06:47.300
I am a bit of...

01:06:47.300 --> 01:06:51.480
No, wait. Stay. Stay a while.

01:06:56.560 --> 01:06:58.200
Me company in my time.

01:07:00.090 --> 01:07:28.420
Are you sure you won't change?

01:08:08.730 --> 01:08:11.310
If you give me a couple of hours, I'll be able to do some research on...

01:08:11.850 --> 01:08:13.190
Who's the man I want to see?

01:08:13.530 --> 01:08:15.150
Hey, Edgar, what's with the SS troops?

01:08:16.230 --> 01:08:17.130
Beyond my control.

01:08:17.960 --> 01:08:20.280
I've got to be at UCLA before 3 o'clock.

01:08:20.420 --> 01:08:21.340
You're the only man I can...

01:08:21.340 --> 01:08:21.700
I'm sorry.

01:08:22.790 --> 01:08:23.370
I'm sorry.

01:08:23.470 --> 01:08:24.730
There's nothing I can do about it.

01:08:25.070 --> 01:08:26.080
Oh, Christ.

01:08:26.470 --> 01:08:28.650
Hey, Aldo, talk some sense into this man, will you?

01:08:29.330 --> 01:08:29.830
Herb's dead.

01:08:30.600 --> 01:08:30.900
What?

01:08:31.590 --> 01:08:33.140
Like the others.

01:08:34.240 --> 01:08:34.860
Oh, my God.

01:08:42.820 --> 01:08:46.660
Okay, Agar, give me one good reason why in the hell any one of us should stay in this place.

01:08:46.860 --> 01:08:47.760
Calm down, Stan.

01:08:48.190 --> 01:08:51.200
Just sit for a minute and listen.

01:08:57.790 --> 01:08:59.130
You're the geneticist, aren't you?

01:09:01.400 --> 01:09:02.900
Can you cross a man and a horse?

01:09:03.840 --> 01:09:04.640
You're asking me?

01:09:05.490 --> 01:09:05.690
Yes.

01:09:06.590 --> 01:09:09.790
Don't you get a centaur, mythologically speaking?

01:09:10.780 --> 01:09:13.460
Realistically speaking, you'd get a summons for bestiality.

01:09:13.970 --> 01:09:16.170
Come on, Agar, what's with the asinine questions?

01:09:16.390 --> 01:09:18.660
Look, look, Williams, I'm serious.

01:09:23.580 --> 01:09:24.940
Now, what about controlled mutations?

01:09:26.060 --> 01:09:29.640
We're not ready or able scientifically to have the kind of controls necessary.

